# EVERYTHING IS LF UNLESS GIT SEES BINARY, added 2026-08-26 on the owner's ruling. The blocks below
# stay exactly as they were written: they are no longer the mechanism, they are the record of what
# each class cost, and deleting them would delete the only evidence for this line.
#
# WHY THE LIST STOPPED BEING THE ANSWER. It was extended four times, and every extension was a hole
# found the expensive way: `*.ps1` after an edit script's LF anchors matched nothing and cost a
# debugging pass; the six `_schema.yaml` after a `git stash` round-trip turned all of them CRLF;
# `*.tsv` after git rewrote every coverage sidecar and turned 317 of 411 verdicts into BAD VERDICT
# lines; `Makefile` earlier today, announced by git while it was being committed. The defect was
# never the missing class. It was that a per-extension list has holes by construction and nothing
# enumerates them.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE THIS LINE, 2026-08-26: 40 tracked files carried no eol attribute, and two of them
# were the SAME class the first block below was written for - `Dockerfile`, whose RUN lines execute
# in a Linux container, and `docker-compose.yml`, which the `*.yaml` pattern does not reach because
# `.yml` is a different extension. Neither had cost anything yet, and both were one checkout away
# from costing the thing that block names.
#
# THIS IS A DEPARTURE FROM "PIN A CLASS AFTER IT HAS COST SOMETHING", AND THE OWNER TOOK IT
# DELIBERATELY. That principle is written against pinning ONE class speculatively. A default is the
# other shape: it inverts the question from which classes to pin into which to exempt, and its own
# cost is on the counter four times over.
#
# `text=auto` AND NOT A BARE `text`: git decides by looking for a NUL byte, so images, fonts and any
# other binary stay untouched. Verified with `git ls-files --eol` across the tree rather than
# assumed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two files of `vault/wiki/` that only gain lines, merged as the union of both sides: at a
# merge of `main` into a sprint branch by `/merge-main`, git keeps the lines of each branch
# and writes no conflict into them.
vault/wiki/log.md merge=union
vault/wiki/tags.md merge=union

# Скрипты, исполняемые внутри Linux-контейнера, обязаны иметь LF:
# CRLF ломает строку запуска и даёт "no such file or directory" на entrypoint.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown is the vault's storage format and is parsed by awk and bash, on the host
# and in the container alike: a CR is a byte inside the field, not a line ending.
# Without this line every .md follows core.autocrlf and depends on what wrote it last.
*.md text eol=lf

# PowerShell hooks and JSON config. The index already holds LF for these - core.autocrlf
# normalises on the way in - so pinning changes nothing about the repository and everything
# about the WORKING copy, which is what scripts that edit these files actually read. Added
# 2026-08-17, after an edit script's LF anchors matched nothing in a CRLF .ps1 and cost a
# debugging pass. This project pins a class after it has cost something, never before.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# The per-folder schemas. Same class as the three above and missed only because it did not exist
# when they were written: `_schema.yaml` is the gate's own input, read by awk and bash exactly as
# the .md corpus is, so a CR is a byte inside the field here too. Added 2026-08-19, after a
# `git stash` round-trip turned all six CRLF and an edit script's LF anchors matched nothing in
# them - the same failure, on the same day the files became load-bearing. This project pins a
# class after it has cost something, and this one now has.
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Python entry points, added 2026-08-24 with the transcript reader and its selftest. THIS ONE IS
# PINNED ON A COST PAID BY ITS SIBLING RATHER THAN BY ITSELF, which is a departure from the line
# every other block here ends with, and the owner ruled it deliberately. `*.ps1` was pinned on
# 2026-08-17 after an edit script's LF anchors matched nothing in a CRLF file and cost a debugging
# pass; `sed -i` with LF anchors was run against `scripts/session-dialogue.py` twice on the day it
# was written, and worked only because the working copy was still LF. After one checkout it would
# not have been. The principle exists against SPECULATIVE pinning, and this failure is measured -
# on the same mechanism, one file class over.
*.py text eol=lf

# The coverage sidecars. A `.tsv` is read field-by-field by awk exactly as the .md corpus is, and a
# CR is a byte inside the verdict rather than a line ending. This one has already cost the most of
# any of them: git rewrote every sidecar to CRLF and turned 317 of 411 verdicts into BAD VERDICT
# lines. `vault-coverage.sh` has carried its own CR strip on both fields ever since - so the
# defence lived in the script and not in the class list, which is the gap this closes. Added
# 2026-08-20, on the owner's ruling, after git warned about the class again while committing the
# ingest that raised it. This project pins a class after it has cost something, and this one has
# cost the most.
*.tsv text eol=lf

# The Makefile, added 2026-08-26 on the owner's ruling, in the same session that wrote it. A recipe
# line is an invocation line: `make` hands it to `sh`, and a CR at its end travels into the command,
# which is the failure `*.sh` was pinned for at the top of this file in exactly those words. The
# file has no extension, so no pattern above reaches it and `git check-attr` reported `unspecified`.
#
# PINNED BEFORE IT COST ANYTHING, WHICH IS THE SECOND DEPARTURE FROM THE LINE THE BLOCKS ABOVE END
# WITH, and it is deliberate rather than an oversight. Git said it outright while the file was being
# committed - "in the working copy of 'Makefile', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git
# touches it" - so the conversion is announced rather than hypothetical, and the blob and working
# copy were both measured clean at that moment. What is new is the stake: this file became the
# single entry point for every check in the repository on the day it was written, so the first
# checkout that converted it would break `make` for everything at once, with the fastest reader of
# the tree unable to run.
Makefile text eol=lf
